---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import QuoteForm from "../../components/QuoteForm.astro";
import FAQ from "../../components/FAQ.astro";
import CTA from "../../components/CTA.astro";
import { serviceSchema } from "../../lib/schema";
import { SITE_URL, PRICE, SUBURBS, SERVICES, GUARANTEE_FULL } from "../../config";

const service = SERVICES.find((s) => s.slug === "spa-barrier-inspection")!;
const url = `${SITE_URL}/services/${service.slug}/`;
const linkedSuburbs = SUBURBS.slice(0, 6).concat(SUBURBS.slice(14, 16));

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Does my spa really need to be inspected like a pool?",
    answer:
      "Yes, if it holds more than 300mm of water. Victorian barrier requirements apply based on water depth, not on whether something is officially called a pool or a spa, so most spas — fixed or portable — fall squarely within scope.",
  },
  {
    question: "What about small, portable spas — do they count?",
    answer:
      "If a portable spa can hold more than 300mm of water when filled, it needs to be registered and barrier-compliant the same as a fixed spa or pool, regardless of how easily it could be moved or emptied.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can the spa's own walls act as the barrier?",
    answer:
      "In some cases, yes — similar to above-ground pools, a spa's rigid walls can form part of the compliance barrier provided they meet the height requirement and don't have a fixed step or ladder giving constant external access.",
  },
  {
    question: "What if my spa is on a deck or elevated area?",
    answer:
      "Decking and elevated positioning need to be assessed together with the spa itself, since the surrounding deck rail or barrier needs to meet the same height and non-climbable requirements as a ground-level fence.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need a separate Form 23 if I have both a pool and a spa?",
    answer:
      "If they share one continuous barrier, a single inspection and Form 23 can often cover both. If the spa has its own separate barrier, it may need its own assessment — your inspector can confirm which applies to your setup.",
  },
];
---
<Layout
  title={service.metaTitle}
  description={service.metaDescription}
  path={`/services/${service.slug}/`}
  schema={[serviceSchema(service, url)]}
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: "Services", path: "/services/" }, { name: service.name, path: `/services/${service.slug}/` }]} />

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-navy-900 mb-4 max-w-3xl">{service.h1}</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-navy-600 max-w-2xl mb-8">{service.summary}</p>

    <div class="prose-content max-w-3xl">
      <p>
        Spas are one of the most commonly overlooked parts of Victorian pool safety law. Owners frequently assume
        that because a spa is smaller than a pool, above-ground, or even inflatable, it must sit outside the barrier
        compliance rules that apply to a full-size swimming pool. It doesn't. Once a spa holds more than 300mm of
        water, it's treated the same as a swimming pool for registration and barrier compliance purposes — including
        the requirement for a Form 23 Certificate of Barrier Compliance and the four-year re-inspection cycle.
      </p>

      <h2>What counts as a spa under Victorian rules</h2>
      <p>
        The rule is based on water depth, not on what the product is marketed as or how permanent it is. That means
        the requirement can apply to:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Fixed, plumbed-in spas built into decking or paving.</li>
        <li>Above-ground, freestanding spa pools and plunge pools.</li>
        <li>Portable or inflatable spas, provided they hold more than 300mm of water when filled.</li>
        <li>Spas that share a barrier with an adjoining swimming pool.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A common mistake is assuming a spa is exempt because it can be emptied, moved, or packed away. The
        requirement is about the water depth it's capable of holding in normal use, not about its portability.
      </p>

      <h2>What a spa barrier inspection checks</h2>
      <p>
        The assessment mirrors a standard pool inspection: barrier height (minimum 1200mm), gaps under and within the
        fence, the non-climbable zone around the barrier's outside face, and gate self-closing and self-latching
        function. For above-ground and portable spas, we specifically check whether the spa's own rigid wall can
        serve as part of the barrier, and whether any fixed steps or ladders compromise that by providing constant
        external access.
      </p>
    </div>

    <CTA
      heading={`Book your spa barrier inspection — ${PRICE} flat`}
      subtext="Same flat fee and re-inspection guarantee as a full pool inspection."
    />

    <div class="prose-content max-w-3xl">
      <h2>Registration and the four-year cycle apply too</h2>
      <p>
        Spas need to be registered with the local council the same way pools do, and once certified, the Form 23 is
        valid for four years before a fresh inspection is required. There's no lighter-touch version of this cycle
        for spas — the same rules, the same penalties for non-compliance (which can exceed $1,600), and the same
        30-day lodgement window after a certificate is issued.
      </p>

      <h2>Pricing</h2>
      <p>
        Spa barrier inspections are charged at the same {PRICE} flat fee as a standard pool inspection, including
        your Form 23 on a pass. {GUARANTEE_FULL} See our full <a href="/pricing/">pricing breakdown</a> for details.
      </p>

      <h2>Related guides</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/guides/form-23-certificate-guide/">The complete Form 23 guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/register-pool-with-council/">How to register a pool or spa with council</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/what-fails-pool-inspection/">What commonly fails a barrier inspection</a></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Available across Melbourne</h2>
      <p>We inspect spa barriers across the metro area, including:</p>
      <ul class="columns-2 sm:columns-3">
        {linkedSuburbs.map((s) => (
          <li><a href={`/locations/${s.slug}/`}>Spa inspections {s.name}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p><a href="/locations/">See all service areas &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>

    <div id="quote-form" class="max-w-3xl mx-auto my-12">
      <QuoteForm />
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <FAQ faqs={faqs} heading="Frequently asked questions about spa barrier inspections" />
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
